ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186543020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543435 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bf80bec0-685f-5049-8ac7-2700161ad366" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543446 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186542978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543446 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186543072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543446 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543446 #edb/ref 1077 "field-4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543446 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186542978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543446 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d5533b86-b75f-5b4f-b691-ab7895f8ca87" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543447 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186542787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543447 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186542956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543447 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186543273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543447 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543447 #edb/ref 1077 "field-5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543447 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186542787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543447 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "de5e06b2-261b-5cb7-abf6-2181d50769ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543449 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186543042 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543449 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186543066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543449 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186543115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543449 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543449 #edb/ref 1077 "field-8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543449 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186543066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543449 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e03a9158-a71a-5896-8c5a-8cd1ffb41264" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543481 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543312 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543481 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543481 #edb/ref 1069 "q-355845" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543481 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543481 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $3$ features in the form of the $5\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-0.6 & -0.95 & -1.02 \\\\ 0.79 & 0.33 & 1.56 \\\\ -1.48 & -1.17 & -0.66 \\\\ 0.67 & 0.8 & -0.15 \\\\ 0.63 & 0.99 & 0.27\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.462 & 0.174 & -0.685 & 0.1 & 0.527 \\\\ 0.473 & -0.718 & -0.24 & 0.358 & 0.272 \\\\ -0.613 & -0.29 & 0.546 & 0.457 & 0.181 \\\\ 0.253 & 0.517 & -0.053 & 0.798 & -0.17 \\\\ 0.35 & 0.319 & 0.416 & -0.127 & 0.766\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}3.171 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.32 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0.477 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.615 & 0.231 & -0.754 \\\\ 0.587 & 0.505 & 0.633 \\\\ 0.527 & -0.832 & 0.175\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on \n{{field-1}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $95%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543481 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1c7b66a2-e4ef-5d7b-9466-353945dad93a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543481 #edb/ref 1074 "In reducing the dimensionality of the dataset, we aim to preserve as much of the original variance as possible. \n\nWe should pick components based on $\\text{the amount of cumulative variance preserved}.$\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\nIn our example, the singular values are\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=3.171,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=1.32,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=0.477.\n$$\n\nLet us now compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.171}^{2}}{{3.171}^{2}+{1.32}^{2}+{0.477}^{2}}≈83.6%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.171}^{2}+{1.32}^{2}}{{3.171}^{2}+{1.32}^{2}+{0.477}^{2}}≈98.1%.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain that\n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $83.6%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $98.1%$ of the variance.\n\n![](images/1d/1d967881aaa7c2a7cd7f537d70d3637edae36b3edf62e15ac67949925d5ee2ef.png)\n\nTherefore, we should reduce the dataset to $2\\text{ dimensions},$ since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $95%$ of the variance." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543515 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543515 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543515 #edb/ref 1069 "q-355902" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543515 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543515 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $4$ features in the form of the $5\\times 4$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0.93 & 0.98 & -1.44 & -1.13 \\\\ -0.14 & -0.96 & 0.96 & 1.58 \\\\ -1.06 & -1.09 & -0.61 & -0.05 \\\\ -0.86 & 0.96 & 0.42 & 0.08 \\\\ 1.12 & 0.1 & 0.68 & -0.48\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.725 & 0.1 & -0.166 & -0.483 & 0.452 \\\\ 0.64 & -0.269 & -0.185 & -0.532 & 0.449 \\\\ 0.207 & 0.729 & -0.311 & 0.363 & 0.444 \\\\ 0.032 & 0.06 & 0.889 & 0.076 & 0.446 \\\\ -0.149 & -0.619 & -0.227 & 0.588 & 0.444\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}3.067 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.933 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1.517 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0.763 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.384 & -0.717 & -0.539 & -0.218 \\\\ -0.5 & -0.229 & 0.78 & -0.297 \\\\ 0.471 & -0.643 & 0.31 & 0.518 \\\\ 0.617 & -0.141 & 0.06 & -0.772\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on \n{{field-1}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543515 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6d89bf53-876c-5233-a22b-c8fa7b16fa9b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543515 #edb/ref 1074 "In reducing the dimensionality of the dataset, we aim to preserve as much of the original variance as possible. \n\nWe should pick components based on $\\text{the amount of cumulative variance preserved}.$\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\nIn our example, the singular values are\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=3.067,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=1.933,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=1.517,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=0.763.\n$$\n\nLet us now compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.067}^{2}}{{3.067}^{2}+{1.933}^{2}+{1.517}^{2}+{0.763}^{2}}≈58.7%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.067}^{2}+{1.933}^{2}}{{3.067}^{2}+{1.933}^{2}+{1.517}^{2}+{0.763}^{2}}≈82.0%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first three principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.067}^{2}+{1.933}^{2}+{1.517}^{2}}{{3.067}^{2}+{1.933}^{2}+{1.517}^{2}+{0.763}^{2}}≈96.4%.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain that\n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $58.7%$ of the variance,\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $82.0%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $3$ dimensions would preserve $96.4%$ of the variance.\n\n![](images/ff/ff1aa60e989377c3767643d2538755d932bc40669f550b57040581192a104f8c.png)\n\nTherefore, we should reduce the dataset to $3\\text{ dimensions},$ since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $90%$ of the variance." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543563 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543563 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543563 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543563 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543563 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543563 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543563 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543563 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543563 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543563 #edb/ref 1069 "q-355958" #edb/ref 175921860454